  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The pandemic exposed the fragility and vulnerability of the private sector social security schemes in Sri Lanka. The Employers Federation of Ceylon (EFC) undertook a study to ascertain the issues and challenges that impede social security schemes to be more beneficial to workers and respond better to enterprise sustainability and job protection. 
To develop the study, EFC conducted a survey among its members, a cross section of workers, and government officials. The survey reached over 750 respondents, between December 2020 and January 2021. 
The findings of the survey revealed that, based on the current contributions made by employers and workers to these schemes, benefits can be expanded to include maternity benefits, payment of wages during a crisis among other things. It also revealed that there is an imminent need for reforms in the governance structure of these funds with representation from Employers and Trade Unions.
The EFC launched the survey-based report was developed by a research team at the Institute of Policy Studies of Sri Lanka led by the Director in charge of research and the EFC staff. The EFC launched the report at a Tripartite meeting on 30th June 2021 and thereafter submitted the report and recommendations to the Ministry of Labour. The Minister of Labour included the subject of Social Security at its following National Labour Advisory Council Meeting on 9th August 2021.
As a follow-up to the EFC recommendations, the Minister of Labour set up a tripartite committee to explore possibilities of reforms in Social security. As a member of the committee, EFC is able to pursue its recommendations for reforms in social security, in Sri Lanka by their participation in the Committee. The ILO supported the study on repositioning social security in Sri Lanka by formulating the concept note, drafting the questionnaires for the different stakeholders and arranging 15 Key Informant Interviews. The ILO supported the analysis of the information in consultation with EFC and assisted the finalization of the EFC&apos;s social security report. The consultations  with EFC and the research team focussed on highlighting issues flowing from the findings and making concrete recommendations so that EFC could develop proposals for  social security reforms.  The report and the recommendations were formally accepted and endorsed by the Director General of EFC at a Tripartite Validation Workshop organised by ILO. The objective of the workshop was to disseminate  the findings  to all stakeholders including the Ministry Of Labour and Trade Unions. More than 50 participants representing all three constituencies attended the validation workshop, moderated by ILO. During the discussions, the Trade Unions&apos; and the Employers&apos; representatives agreed that there is much potential for better and efficient management of the funds that could result in better benefits for workers.
The ILO also followed up with EFC on the setting up of the new committee by the Ministry of Labour to explore reforms in Social security. The ILO provided technical advice to EFC on steering the reforms in line with the recommendations.</narrative>
   </description>
